 ## Overview
-This method provides direct haptic rendering of implicit isosurfaces within volumetric data using a point-based haptic device, eliminating the need to extract polygonal meshes [S1]. It employs a virtual plane as an intermediate geometric representation to decouple the visual rendering process from the high-frequency haptic control loop, thereby maintaining stability and high servo rates for complex volumetric data [S1, S3]. Unlike methods that approximate stiffness via density fields or require expensive gradient computations per loop, this approach uses a locally approximated tangential plane to compute interaction forces, offering a balance between computational efficiency and realistic contact sensation [S1, S3].
+This method provides direct haptic rendering of implicit isosurfaces within volumetric data using a point-based haptic device, eliminating the need to extract polygonal meshes [S1]. It employs a virtual plane as an intermediate geometric representation to decouple the visual rendering process from the high-frequency haptic control loop, thereby maintaining stability and high servo rates for complex volumetric data [S1]. Unlike methods that approximate stiffness via density fields or require expensive gradient computations per loop, this approach uses a locally approximated tangential plane to compute interaction forces, offering a balance between computational efficiency and realistic contact sensation [S1].
 
